Chapter: TrigSpec
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils
  cites Safari chapter Trig
  cites Judge chapter Grade

 Trig, checked against values it does not compute.

 THIS IS A SPEC, NOT A JUDGE, and the difference is where the expected values
 come from. Everything in judge/ grades this port against the ORIGINAL GAME:
 a zig probe calls wasm/*.zig, writes gold/, and the check maps over it. That
 answers "is the port faithful", it needs the game to be present, and for a
 chapter like Render it costs six hundred million steps because a scene has to
 be driven before there is anything to compare.

 A spec answers a smaller question and answers it in thousands of steps: does
 this chapter still compute what it is supposed to compute. The expected values
 are LITERALS, written here, taken from the mathematical definition rather than
 from any implementation -- so this file is the whole test, and any arm that can
 run Codex can render a verdict from it alone. No gold, no probe, no second
 artifact to keep in step, and the same file is what bare metal, the zig plug,
 the wasm plug and the Rust interpreter each run.

 TRIG HAD NO CHECK AT ALL BEFORE THIS. Thirty-five of the port's fifty-four
 chapters do not, and this is the first of them: `r-sin` and `r-cos` are called
 by ten chapters and graded by none, so an error outside the domains those ten
 happen to sample was invisible.

 We say:

Section: The Angles

 Nine angles across two full turns, chosen to cross every quadrant and to land
 on none of the exact points where a series implementation is flattered -- the
 whole turn, the half turn and the right angle are all absent on purpose.

  ang : List Real
  ang = [0.0, 0.3, 0.7, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0, 3.0, 4.5, 6.0]

  sin-want : List Real
  sin-want =
    [ 0.0,
      0.29552020666133955,
      0.644217687237691,
      0.8414709848078965,
      0.9974949866040544,
      0.9092974268256817,
      0.1411200080598672,
      -0.977530117665097,
      -0.27941549819892586 ]

  cos-want : List Real
  cos-want =
    [ 1.0,
      0.955336489125606,
      0.7648421872844885,
      0.5403023058681398,
      0.0707372016677029,
      -0.4161468365471424,
      -0.9899924966004454,
      -0.2107957994307797,
      0.960170286650366 ]

Section: The Arc Tangent

 `r-atan` is a halving-and-series construction rather than a table, so it is
 graded on its own inputs: the values where the halving count changes are the
 ones worth having.

  atan-in : List Real
  atan-in = [0.0, 0.25, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 4.0]

  atan-want : List Real
  atan-want =
    [ 0.0,
      0.24497866312686414,
      0.4636476090008061,
      0.7853981633974483,
      1.1071487177940904,
      1.3258176636680326 ]

Section: Entry

 The tolerance is the claim. A spec that passes at a tolerance nobody measured
 says only that the number is in the right neighbourhood, and this port's own
 FINDINGS sends upstream a docstring claiming 0.1% where the code delivers
 0.45% -- an unmeasured accuracy claim is a defect wherever it is written.

 SO THESE THREE WERE MEASURED, by tightening each until it broke:

   r-sin    passes 5e-8, fails 2e-8    -- about 3e-8
   r-cos    passes 1e-7, fails 5e-8    -- about 7e-8
   r-atan   passes 5e-16               -- double precision, near enough

 The tolerances below are those numbers rounded out one step, so a regression
 in any of the three trips this file rather than being absorbed by slack.

 R-COS IS WORST AT THE ZERO ANGLE, which is index 0 and not a coincidence.
 `cos 0.0` should be exactly 1.0 and the series returns it about 7e-8 short,
 because there is no special case at zero. Every caller in this port feeds it
 an angle that has already been through a lean or a heading, so none of them
 sees the exact case; a chapter that asks for the cosine of nothing does.

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "t-sin " (list-map r-sin ang) sin-want 0.0000001)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "t-cos " (list-map r-cos ang) cos-want 0.0000002)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "t-atan" (list-map r-atan atan-in) atan-want 0.000000000000001)
  end
